const e=[{id:"sv-1-279",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"For the business case, a Green Belt sizes the cost of SLA service credits. Last quarter the operator paid credits on **400** enterprise SLA breaches, at an average of **OMR 150** per breach. Assuming the quarter is typical, what **annual** cost should the business case quote?",options:["OMR 60,000","OMR 180,000","OMR 240,000","OMR 720,000"],answer:2,hint:"Annual cost = events per period × periods per year × cost per event. Check the period of the count first.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"400 breaches **per quarter**; OMR 150 per breach; a year has **4** quarters."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Annual cost}=400\\times 4\\times 150=240{,}000$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The business case quotes **OMR 240,000 a year** as the current cost of the problem — an early COPQ estimate that Finance will validate later."}],final:"Annual credit cost = OMR 240,000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is one quarter only (400 × 150); it was not annualised.","Multiplied by 3 instead of 4 — a year has four quarters.","","Treated the quarterly count as a monthly count and multiplied by 12."],trap:"Check the time unit of the count (month, quarter, year) before annualising.",bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-280",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`To scope a new project, a Green Belt runs a tally of last quarter’s **billing complaints**:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Complaint reason

Complaint reason   Count  Percent  CumPct
Roaming charges      312    44.57   44.57
Bundle expiry        188    26.86   71.43
Late refund           96    13.71   85.14
Wrong address         54     7.71   92.86
Other                 50     7.14  100.00
N=                   700
\`\`\`

The Champion wants **one** project that covers **at least 70%** of complaints with the **narrowest** scope. Which scope fits?`,options:["Roaming charges only","Roaming charges and bundle expiry","Roaming charges, bundle expiry and late refunds","All five complaint reasons"],answer:1,hint:"Read the cumulative percent (CumPct) column: take the fewest categories whose cumulative share reaches the target.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto-style tally helps scope a charter: focus on the **vital few** categories that carry most of the problem, and list the rest as out of scope."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Roaming alone gives **44.57%** — below 70%. Adding bundle expiry gives a CumPct of **71.43%**, which reaches the target with only two categories."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Three categories (85.14%) also pass 70% but are wider than needed. All five categories is a “boil-the-ocean” scope."}],final:"CumPct 71.43% with two categories → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Roaming alone covers only 44.57% of complaints — below the 70% target.","","This reaches 85.14%, but it is wider than needed: the target is met with two categories.","Taking every category makes the scope too broad — the opposite of a focused charter."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-281",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt pulls SIM-activation data for **July–September 2026** (target: active within **4 hours**):

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Channel, Within 4h

Rows: Channel   Columns: Within 4h

           No    Yes    All
Online     46    374    420
Retail    221    159    380
All       267    533    800
\`\`\`

Which **problem statement** is BEST supported by this output?`,options:["Retail staff are too slow because they are not trained on the activation system, so 58% of retail activations are late.","We need to move retail SIM activations to online self-service to meet the 4-hour target.","SIM activation is often slow in some channels, which annoys customers.","From July to September 2026, 221 of 380 retail SIM activations (58%) took longer than the 4-hour target, against 11% for online activations."],answer:3,hint:"A problem statement says what, where, when and how big — with data — and has no cause, no solution and no blame.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:`Retail: 221 of 380 were **not** within 4 hours. Online: 46 of 420.

$$\\text{Retail late}=\\dfrac{221}{380}=0.58=58\\%$$

$$\\text{Online late}=\\dfrac{46}{420}=0.11=11\\%$$`},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"The last option states **what** (activations over 4 h), **where** (retail vs online), **when** (Jul–Sep 2026) and **how big** (221 of 380, 58%). It names no cause and no fix."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other drafts hide a cause and blame (“not trained”), a solution (“move to online”) or give no data at all (“often slow”)."}],final:"Data-based, no cause or solution → answer **D**",whyWrong:["It contains an unproven cause and blames staff (“not trained”) — causes belong in Analyze, not in the problem statement.","This is a disguised solution: it jumps to “self-service” before Measure and Analyze.","Vague: no where, when or how big, and no number from the data.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-282",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`The business case must state the **annual** cost of SLA credits. Finance supplies twelve months of data:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Monthly SLA credits (OMR)

Variable   N     Mean   StDev  Minimum   Median  Maximum       Sum
Credits   12  18450.0  3120.0  13200.0  18100.0  24600.0  221400.0
\`\`\`

Which figure should the business case quote?`,options:["OMR 221,400 — the Sum","OMR 18,450 — the Mean","OMR 295,200 — the Maximum × 12","OMR 217,200 — the Median × 12"],answer:0,hint:"Annual cost of twelve monthly values = their total (Sum), which also equals Mean × 12.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The business case needs the **actual annual** cost. With twelve monthly values, that is simply the **Sum** (equal to the Mean × 12)."},{title:"Read the output",body:"$$\\text{Annual}=18{,}450\\times 12=221{,}400$$ — matching the Sum column."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Mean is one month. Maximum × 12 assumes every month was the worst month. Median × 12 is not the real total."}],final:"Annual credits = OMR 221,400 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The Mean is the cost of an average month, not the year.","Multiplying the worst month by 12 inflates the business case — it assumes every month was the maximum.","Median × 12 is not the actual total; the real annual cost is the Sum of the twelve months."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-283",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`A draft charter reads:

- **Problem:** *In H1 2026, 31% of enterprise port-in requests missed the 3-working-day window.*
- **Goal:** *Raise the porting customer-satisfaction score to 4.5 out of 5 by 31 March 2027.*

What is the MAIN weakness of this charter?`,options:["The goal has no deadline","The goal uses a different metric from the problem statement","The problem statement contains a root cause","The problem statement gives no time period"],answer:1,hint:"The goal (objective) statement uses the SAME metric as the problem statement, plus a target and a date.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The goal should move the metric that defines the problem. Here the problem is measured as **% of port-ins missing 3 days**, but the goal targets a **satisfaction score**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A better goal: *Reduce enterprise port-ins missing the 3-day window from 31% to 8% by 31 March 2027.* Satisfaction can be tracked, but as a secondary metric."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The goal has a date (31 March 2027); the problem has a period (H1 2026) and no cause."}],final:"Problem and goal use different metrics → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The goal does have a deadline — 31 March 2027.","","The problem statement states no cause; it is factual and measured.","The problem statement does give a period: H1 2026."],trap:"Different metrics in problem and goal statements is a classic charter mistake.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-284",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Three weeks into Measure, a Green Belt cannot get system logs from the IT security department, which says the request is “not a priority”. The project is now stalled. According to the roles in the charter, who is **primarily** responsible for removing this barrier?",options:["The Finance representative","The process owner of the activation process","The Champion (project sponsor)","The Master Black Belt"],answer:2,hint:"The Champion owns the project result, signs the charter and removes organisational barriers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In the charter, the **Champion / sponsor** owns the result, approves the charter and **removes barriers** the Belt cannot remove alone, such as access to another department’s data or people."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The block is organisational (another department’s priorities), so the Green Belt escalates to the Champion."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Finance validates benefits; the process owner runs the process and keeps the gains; the MBB coaches on method. None of them owns cross-department priorities for this project."}],final:"Barrier removal is the Champion’s job → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Finance representative independently evaluates savings; removing barriers is not their role.","The process owner manages the activation process day to day, but has no authority over the IT security department.","","The MBB coaches the Belt on methods and tools; the sponsor role is held by the Champion."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-285",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"The charter for a porting project says: *“The project must be completed with **no new headcount** and **no capital spend**.”* In the risks / assumptions / constraints section, how should this line be classified?",options:["Risk","Assumption","Out-of-scope item","Constraint"],answer:3,hint:"Risk = uncertain event; assumption = treated as true without proof; constraint = fixed limit that must be respected.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **constraint** is a fixed limit the project must respect. A **risk** is an uncertain event that could hurt the project. An **assumption** is something treated as true without proof."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“No new headcount and no capital” is a **fixed limit** set by management — nothing uncertain about it, and nothing to verify. It is a constraint."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Out-of-scope items are process boundaries (products, regions, steps), not limits on resources."}],final:"A fixed resource limit is a constraint → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A risk is an uncertain event (e.g. “the vendor may be late”); this limit is certain.","An assumption is something believed true but unproven; this is a stated rule, not a belief.","Scope lists process boundaries (which products, steps, regions), not resource limits.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-286",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A new Green Belt drafts this goal: *“Reduce **all** customer complaints across **all** products, channels and regions by 50% within **4 months**.”* Her coach says the charter is not workable. What is the BEST next step?",options:["Use complaint data (e.g. a Pareto) to narrow the scope to one manageable process or complaint type, and list the rest as out of scope","Keep the scope and add more team members so the work can be shared","Keep the scope but extend the deadline to 12 months","Keep the scope and drop the 50% target, so there is no risk of missing it"],answer:0,hint:"A Green Belt charter needs a focused, manageable scope; data shows where to focus.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"This is a **“boil-the-ocean”** scope: too wide for one project to analyse properly. The fix is to **narrow the scope** with data and state clear in-scope / out-of-scope boundaries."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A Pareto of complaints shows which product, channel or reason carries most of the problem. The charter then targets that slice; other slices can become later projects."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"More people or more time do not fix an unfocused problem, and dropping the target makes the goal unmeasurable."}],final:"Narrow the scope with data → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","More people do not fix an unfocused scope — the problem is still too broad to analyse.","A longer timeline keeps the same boil-the-ocean scope and delays results.","Removing the target breaks the SMART goal and does nothing about the scope."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-287",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A charter has a business case, problem statement, goal, team list and milestones. Within weeks, managers keep asking the team to “also look at” **prepaid** top-ups, **corporate** accounts and **wholesale** billing. Which missing charter element would MOST likely have prevented this?",options:["A stakeholder list","A scope section listing what is in and out of scope","A risk register","A secondary metric"],answer:1,hint:"Scope draws the fence around the project: what is in and what is out.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **scope** section (in scope / out of scope) is the main defence against **scope creep**. With it written and signed, any addition becomes a formal Champion decision."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Requests to add prepaid, corporate and wholesale billing are classic scope creep. An explicit out-of-scope list would have answered them at once."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Stakeholders, risks and secondary metrics are useful, but none of them sets the project boundary."}],final:"In/out-of-scope section is missing → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A stakeholder list shows who to inform or consult; it does not limit what the project covers.","","A risk register lists uncertain events; it does not fix the project boundary.","A secondary metric is a guard-rail on side effects, not a boundary on the process."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-288",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A deployment lead must assign one candidate to a **Green Belt** working part-time inside the enterprise billing team. Which candidate is the BEST fit?",options:["Redesign the end-to-end order-to-cash process across six departments in all regions","Replace the billing platform with a new vendor system","Fix a broken link in the daily billing report that IT has already diagnosed","Reduce the rate of re-issued enterprise invoices; data is in the billing system and the process sits within the Belt’s own team"],answer:3,hint:"A good Green Belt project: a measurable problem with unknown cause, data available, scope within the Belt’s reach, a few months long.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Green Belt project should have a **measurable problem**, an **unknown root cause**, **available data**, and a scope the Belt can influence — usually **4–6 months** within their own area."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Invoice re-issues meet all four tests. The cross-functional order-to-cash redesign is a Black Belt (or programme) scope."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Replacing the platform is a pre-chosen **solution** (an IT project), and the broken link has a known cause and fix — a **just-do-it**."}],final:"Focused, data-rich, unknown cause, within reach → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Six departments and all regions make this too large and cross-functional for a part-time Green Belt — it suits a Black Belt or a programme.","This starts from a solution (new platform), not a problem with an unknown cause — it is an IT project, not DMAIC.","Cause and fix are already known: just do it; no DMAIC project is needed.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-289",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Champion reviews this goal: *“Reduce repeat calls from 22% to 12% by 30 June 2027 **by deploying a chatbot** on the self-care app.”* What is the BEST revision?",options:["Keep it — naming the chatbot makes the goal more Specific","Move the chatbot clause into the problem statement","Remove the chatbot clause: the goal states what and by when; the how comes from Analyze and Improve","Replace “from 22% to 12%” with “significantly”, because the chatbot’s effect is not yet known"],answer:2,hint:"Goal statement = metric + baseline + target + date. It says WHAT, not HOW.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A goal (objective) statement gives the metric, target and date. It must not name the **solution**, because the real cause is not yet known."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The chatbot is a pre-chosen fix. If Analyze shows repeat calls come from, say, wrong billing data, the chatbot would not help. Removing the clause leaves a clean SMART goal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A solution does not belong in the problem statement either, and “significantly” makes the goal unmeasurable."}],final:"Goal says what and when, never how → answer **C**",whyWrong:["“Specific” in SMART means a specific result (metric and target), not a specific solution.","A solution is just as wrong in the problem statement — it becomes a disguised solution.","","“Significantly” is not measurable; this breaks SMART and keeps the hidden solution problem unsolved."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-290",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Any change to customer-contract wording must be approved by the **Legal** team. Legal will not do project work but must review any proposed change. Where should Legal appear in the charter?",options:["In the stakeholder list, as a group to consult","As a core team member","As the Champion","In the out-of-scope list"],answer:0,hint:"Core team = people who do the project work; stakeholders = people who must be informed or consulted.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The charter separates the **core team** (people who do the work) from **stakeholders** (people who must be **informed or consulted** because the project affects them or needs their approval)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Legal does no project work but must review changes, so it is a stakeholder to consult."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Legal does not own the project result (not the Champion), and listing it as out of scope would wrongly exclude a required approval."}],final:"Consulted, not doing the work → stakeholder → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Core team members do the project work; Legal only reviews and approves.","The Champion owns the project result and removes barriers; Legal has neither role here.","Out-of-scope lists process boundaries; Legal’s approval is a required part of any change, not something excluded."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-291",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about updating a project charter is MOST accurate?",options:["Once the Champion signs it at the Define tollgate, the charter is frozen until project closure","The charter is a living document: it is reviewed at tollgates and updated openly as data improves, with scope changes approved by the Champion","The Green Belt may change the scope at any time without approval, as long as the goal is still met","The charter should be rewritten after Analyze so that the problem statement names the verified root cause"],answer:1,hint:"The Measure checklist asks “Has the problem statement changed?” — changes are expected but must be formal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The charter is a **living document**. The book’s Measure checklist asks “Has the Problem Statement changed?”, so updates are expected — but they are made **openly at tollgates**, and scope changes are **Champion decisions**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A frozen charter ignores new data. Silent changes by the Belt are scope creep. And the problem statement never names a cause — the verified causes are reported in the Analyze deliverables."}],final:"Living document, formal updates → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Too rigid: new baseline data often refines the problem or goal, and the book’s Measure checklist expects that check.","","Unapproved scope change is scope creep; scope changes are formal Champion decisions.","A problem statement should never contain a cause, even a verified one; root causes are reported as Analyze results."],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-1-292",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why is a **disguised solution** in a problem statement (e.g. *“We lack an automated routing tool”*) harmful?",options:["It makes the problem statement too long for a one-page charter","It means the business case cannot be quantified","It makes the scope too narrow for a Green Belt project","It pushes the team to build the named fix and skip Measure and Analyze, even if the real cause is something else"],answer:3,hint:"Warning words: lack of, need to, insufficient, implement, a named tool.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A disguised solution sounds like a problem but already contains the answer. The team then builds that answer instead of measuring the problem and **proving the cause**."},{title:"Apply to the example",body:"“We lack a routing tool” assumes routing is the cause. If tickets are really delayed by missing fault data, the new tool will not help. A strong version states the effect: *31% of P2 incidents waited more than 45 minutes for assignment.*"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Length, quantification and scope width are not the core danger."}],final:"Disguised solution skips Measure and Analyze → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Length is not the issue; a short disguised solution is just as harmful.","The cost of the problem can still be quantified; the danger is the pre-selected fix.","The problem is not width but the built-in answer, which skips the data-driven search for causes.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-293",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book glossary, a **problem statement** is:",options:["A short statement of a business situation that bounds and describes the problem the project will solve","A short statement of the goals, timing and expectations of the project","A list of the verified root causes of the defect","A description of the solution the team will implement"],answer:0,hint:"Problem statement = what is wrong (bounds and describes). Objective statement = goals, timing, expectations.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines the **problem statement** as a short statement that **bounds and describes** the business problem. The **objective statement** is the one about goals, timing and expectations."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Root causes are verified in Analyze, and solutions come in Improve — neither belongs in the problem statement."}],final:"Bounds and describes the problem → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This is the glossary definition of the objective (goal) statement, not the problem statement.","Root causes are not known in Define; they are verified in Analyze.","A solution in the problem statement is a disguised solution — a classic mistake."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-294",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"A good **goal statement** in a project charter contains all of the following EXCEPT:",options:["The same metric used in the problem statement","The solution the team will implement to reach the target","A target value for the metric","A completion date"],answer:1,hint:"Goal = same metric + target + date (SMART). No method.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The goal statement uses the **same metric** as the problem statement, adds a **target** and a **date**, and is SMART. It says **what**, not **how**."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Metric, target and date all belong. The solution does not — it is found in Analyze and Improve."}],final:"The solution is NOT part of the goal → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The goal must move the same metric that defines the problem — it belongs.","","A target makes the goal measurable — it belongs.","A date makes the goal time-bound — it belongs."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-295",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Once the Champion signs the project charter at the Define tollgate, it must not be changed for the rest of the project. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"The charter is a living document reviewed at each tollgate.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The charter is a **living document**. As baseline data arrives, the problem, goal or scope may need refining. The book’s Measure checklist even asks “Has the Problem Statement changed?”."},{title:"Apply",body:"Changes are allowed, but they are made **openly** and approved by the Champion — never silently. So the statement is false."}],final:"The charter can be updated formally → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["A frozen charter ignores what the team learns; the book expects the problem statement to be reviewed in Measure.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-1-296",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt classifies four lines for the charter:

| Row | Charter line | Classified as |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | The data vendor may not deliver the call logs on time | Risk |
| 2 | The regulator may change the porting rules during the project | Constraint |
| 3 | Priority codes are applied consistently by all agents | Assumption |
| 4 | No new headcount may be added | Constraint |

Which row is **misclassified**?`,options:["Row 1","Row 2","Row 3","Row 4"],answer:1,hint:"Risk = uncertain event (“may”); assumption = believed true, unproven; constraint = fixed limit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **risk** is an uncertain future event that could hurt the project. A **constraint** is a fixed, certain limit. An **assumption** is treated as true without proof."},{title:"Check each row",body:"Row 1: “may not deliver” — uncertain → risk ✓. Row 2: “**may** change the rules” — uncertain → it is a **risk**, not a constraint ✗. Row 3: believed but unproven → assumption ✓. Row 4: fixed limit → constraint ✓."}],final:"Row 2 is a risk, not a constraint → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Row 1 is correct: a possible late delivery is an uncertain event — a risk.","","Row 3 is correct: consistent coding is believed but not yet verified — an assumption.","Row 4 is correct: a fixed headcount limit is a constraint."],trap:"The word “may” signals uncertainty — a risk.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-297",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A project raises **first-contact resolution (FCR)** from a baseline of **70%** to **84%**. By what **percentage** did FCR improve relative to the baseline?",options:["14%","16.7%","20%","120%"],answer:2,hint:"Relative improvement = change divided by the baseline value, × 100. Percentage points ≠ percent.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Baseline FCR = 70%; new FCR = 84%. Higher is better."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Improvement}=\\dfrac{\\text{New}-\\text{Baseline}}{\\text{Baseline}}\\times 100=\\dfrac{84-70}{70}\\times 100=\\dfrac{14}{70}\\times 100=20\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"FCR rose by **14 percentage points**, which is a **20%** improvement on the baseline."}],final:"20% relative improvement → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is the change in percentage points (84 − 70), not the percentage improvement.","Divided by the new value (14 ÷ 84) instead of the baseline.","","This is the new value as a percent of the baseline (84 ÷ 70 × 100), not the improvement."],trap:"Always divide by the baseline; “points” and “percent” are different.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-298",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"The baseline mean time to provision a business VPN is **25 days**. The Champion sets a goal to **reduce it by 40%**. What target should the goal statement show?",options:["10 days","24.6 days","17.9 days","15 days"],answer:3,hint:"Target = baseline × (1 − reduction fraction).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Baseline = 25 days; required reduction = 40% = 0.40."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Target}=25\\times(1-0.40)=25\\times 0.60=15$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The goal: *reduce mean VPN provisioning time from 25 to 15 days by [date].* Check: $\\dfrac{25-15}{25}=0.40$."}],final:"Target = 15 days → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is the size of the reduction (40% of 25), not the target that remains.","Subtracted 0.40 days instead of 40% of the baseline.","Divided the baseline by 1.40 — that is not a 40% reduction (it is only a 28.6% cut).",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-299",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A project’s primary metric is **failed fibre installations** (known 5 days after the order). The team tests whether the daily count of **orders entered with incomplete address data** could act as an early signal. Data for 60 days:

\`\`\`
Correlation: Incomplete_orders, Failed_installs_day+5

Pearson correlation   0.78
P-value               0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["There is a strong positive association, so incomplete orders are a useful leading indicator to watch; whether they cause failures must still be confirmed in Analyze","Incomplete address data is proven to be the root cause of failed installations","Failed installations are a leading indicator of incomplete orders","With a p-value of 0.000 there is no relationship between the two metrics"],answer:0,hint:"Leading indicator = moves before the outcome. p < 0.05 → significant association; correlation alone does not prove cause.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"r = 0.78 is a strong positive correlation, and p = 0.000 < 0.05, so the association is significant."},{title:"Apply to metrics",body:"Incomplete orders are known **at order entry**, five days **before** the failure — a **leading** indicator. Failed installs are the **lagging** outcome. The team can steer on the leading metric between monthly results."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Correlation shows association, not cause. The p-value is small, which means a significant relationship, not “no relationship”."}],final:"Strong, significant association → useful leading indicator → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Correlation does not prove causation; the cause must be verified in Analyze.","Reversed: the failures happen five days later, so they lag; the incomplete-order count leads.","A small p-value (0.000 < 0.05) means a significant association, not the absence of one."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-300",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A contact-centre project has **average handle time (AHT)** as its primary metric and **first-contact resolution (FCR)** as a secondary metric. 30 days before and after the change:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: AHT_min, FCR_pct

Variable  Period   N    Mean
AHT_min   Before  30    7.82
          After   30    5.94
FCR_pct   Before  30   78.40
          After   30   66.10
\`\`\`

What should the Green Belt conclude?`,options:["Success — AHT fell by about 24%, which meets the goal","Success — both metrics changed, so the process has clearly improved","Not yet a success — the primary metric improved, but the secondary metric (FCR) got worse by about 12 points; investigate before closing","Failure — AHT should have risen if quality improved"],answer:2,hint:"Success = primary metric improves WITHOUT compromising the secondary (guard-rail) metrics.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"$$\\text{AHT change}=\\dfrac{7.82-5.94}{7.82}\\times 100=24.0\\%$$ FCR fell from 78.40% to 66.10% — a drop of 12.3 points."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"Secondary metrics are **guard-rails**. The book’s Control checklist asks whether the primary metric improved **without compromising the secondary metrics**. Shorter calls that leave more issues unresolved only move the work into repeat calls."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A falling FCR is not an improvement; and AHT is not expected to rise."}],final:"Guard-rail breached → not yet a success → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Ignores the secondary metric: FCR fell 12.3 points, so the gain in AHT came at the customer’s expense.","“Changed” is not “improved” — FCR moved in the wrong direction.","","Lower AHT is the goal; the problem is the FCR drop, not the direction of AHT."],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-301",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The CTQ is *“router delivered within **5 days** of order”*. Baseline data for 600 orders:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Delivery time (days)

Variable    N   Mean  StDev  Median    Q3  Maximum
Days      600   3.60   1.40    3.30  4.40     9.00

Count of Days > 5:  108
\`\`\`

Which baseline statement is MOST useful for the charter?`,options:["The mean of 3.6 days is within the 5-day CTQ, so there is no problem to solve","18.0% of orders (108 of 600) were delivered after the 5-day CTQ","The maximum is 9 days, so all deliveries are late","The standard deviation is 1.4 days, so the process is capable"],answer:1,hint:"For a time CTQ, report the share beyond the limit — an average can hide many late units.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"$$\\text{Late share}=\\dfrac{108}{600}=0.18=18.0\\%$$"},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"The customer feels each late delivery, not the average. A mean of 3.6 days hides that almost one order in five breaks the CTQ. The primary metric should be **% of orders over 5 days**, with a baseline of **18.0%**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"One maximum does not make all deliveries late, and a standard deviation alone says nothing about capability without the specification."}],final:"Baseline = 18.0% late → answer **B**",whyWrong:["An average inside the limit can hide many late orders; 108 orders broke the CTQ.","","The maximum is a single order; the median is 3.3 days, so most deliveries are on time.","A standard deviation alone does not show capability; 18% beyond the limit shows it is not capable."],trap:"Mean inside the spec ≠ customers satisfied.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-302",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Before collecting baseline data on **SIM activation time**, a Green Belt finds that the retail team measures from **payment** to **SIM working**, while IT measures from **order entered in the system** to **provisioning complete**. What should she do FIRST?",options:["Average the two teams’ figures to get a combined baseline","Use IT’s figures because system timestamps are more accurate","Collect both sets of data and decide later which one to report","Agree one operational definition of activation time, with clear start and stop points, before collecting the baseline"],answer:3,hint:"A baseline needs an operational definition: exactly what counts, where the clock starts and stops.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A credible baseline starts with an **operational definition** — what exactly is measured, where the clock **starts** and **stops**, and from which data source."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The two teams measure different things. The team (with the process owner) should agree one definition that reflects the **customer’s** experience — here probably payment to SIM working — and then collect the baseline."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Averaging two different measures gives a meaningless number; picking one for convenience or postponing the decision leaves the metric undefined."}],final:"Define the metric first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The two figures measure different intervals; their average describes nothing real.","Accuracy of timestamps does not fix the definition; IT’s interval may not match what the customer experiences.","Postponing the definition wastes data collection and invites later disputes about the baseline.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-303",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Complaints about home broadband rose from **2,400** in H1 to **2,640** in H2, while active subscribers grew from **200,000** to **264,000**. A manager wants to report “complaints up 10%”. Which metric gives the FAIREST comparison?",options:["Total number of complaints per half-year","Complaints per 1,000 active subscribers","Complaints per contact-centre agent","Percentage change in the number of complaints"],answer:1,hint:"Normalise counts by volume (a rate) before comparing periods of different size.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Raw counts mislead when volume changes. A **rate per unit of exposure** makes periods comparable."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"$$\\text{H1}=\\dfrac{2{,}400}{200{,}000}\\times 1{,}000=12.0$$ $$\\text{H2}=\\dfrac{2{,}640}{264{,}000}\\times 1{,}000=10.0$$ The count rose, but the complaint **rate fell** from 12.0 to 10.0 per 1,000 subscribers."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Totals and their percentage change ignore the growth in subscribers; complaints per agent depends on staffing, not on the customer’s experience."}],final:"Use a normalised rate → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Raw totals ignore the 32% growth in subscribers, so they overstate the problem.","","Per-agent figures change with staffing levels, not with the service customers receive.","This is the same raw count in a different form; it still ignores the bigger customer base."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-304",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt proposes **four primary metrics** for one contact-centre project: average handle time, first-contact resolution, customer satisfaction and cost per contact. What is the BEST advice?",options:["Choose one primary metric that directly measures the charter problem, and track the others as secondary (guard-rail) metrics","Keep all four as primary metrics so no improvement is missed","Combine the four into a single weighted index and use it as the only metric","Drop all four and use financial savings as the primary metric"],answer:0,hint:"One project Y at a time; the others become guard-rails.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book advises solving **one output at a time** — chasing several Y’s at once becomes overwhelming. The **primary metric** is the single measure of the charter problem; **secondary metrics** watch for side effects."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If the problem is repeat contacts, FCR is the primary metric, and AHT, satisfaction and cost become guard-rails."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A weighted index hides which part moved, and money is usually a consequence of the primary metric, not the metric itself."}],final:"One primary metric, others secondary → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Four primary Y’s split the analysis; the book recommends one output at a time.","An index blends metrics so the team cannot see which one moved or why.","Savings are calculated from the primary metric’s improvement; they do not replace a process measure."],bookRef:"Book p. 116"},{id:"sv-1-305",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The book glossary describes **breakthrough improvement** as an improvement of about what size over the baseline?",options:["About 10%","About 30%","About 70%","100% — the defect is removed completely"],answer:2,hint:"Breakthrough ≈ at or near 70% over baseline.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary calls **breakthrough improvement** a gain **at or near 70% over baseline**. It reminds teams that Six Sigma targets should be ambitious."},{title:"Apply",body:"Example: cutting a breach rate from 18% to 6% is a $\\dfrac{18-6}{18}=66.7\\%$ improvement — close to breakthrough level."}],final:"Breakthrough ≈ 70% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["About 10% is an incremental gain, far below the breakthrough level.","About 30% is a solid gain, but the glossary sets breakthrough near 70%.","","Breakthrough does not require removing every defect."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-306",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A project aims to improve **monthly SLA compliance** for enterprise fault repair. Which of these is a **process** (in-process) metric rather than an outcome or financial metric?",options:["Monthly SLA compliance %","% of fault tickets logged with a complete fault description","Number of enterprise customers lost per quarter","Service credits paid per month"],answer:1,hint:"Outcome = what the customer or business feels; process = how the work is done; financial = money.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Outcome** metrics show results the customer feels (SLA compliance, churn). **Financial** metrics show money (credits paid). **Process** metrics show how the work is done inside the process — and they often **lead** the outcome."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Complete fault descriptions at logging are a step inside the repair process; poor descriptions lead to slower repairs later."}],final:"Complete-description % is a process metric → answer **B**",whyWrong:["SLA compliance is the outcome the customer feels — and it lags.","","Lost customers (churn) is an outcome metric.","Credits paid is a financial metric."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-307",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Reducing the invoice error rate from **20%** to **15%** is a **5%** improvement. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Percentage improvement divides the change by the baseline.",steps:[{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Improvement}=\\dfrac{20-15}{20}\\times 100=25\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The rate fell by **5 percentage points**, which is a **25%** improvement. The statement mixes up points and percent, so it is false."}],final:"5 points = 25% improvement → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["5 is the change in percentage points; the relative improvement is 25%.",""],trap:"Points vs percent.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-308",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A project avoids **500** repeat field visits a month, at **OMR 40** per visit. **30%** of these visits were done by a contractor paid per visit; **70%** by in-house staff, whose headcount and overtime do not change. What **annual hard saving** should Finance record?",options:["OMR 6,000","OMR 168,000","OMR 240,000","OMR 72,000"],answer:3,hint:"Hard saving = only the part that reduces actual spend. Annual = monthly × 12.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Avoided visits 500 a month; OMR 40 each; only the **contractor** share (30%) cuts real spending."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Hard saving}=500\\times 0.30\\times 40\\times 12=72{,}000$$"},{title:"Classify the rest",body:"The in-house 70% frees staff time but removes no cost: $500\\times 0.70\\times 40\\times 12=168{,}000$ is a **soft** saving."}],final:"Hard saving = OMR 72,000 a year → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is the monthly hard saving; it was not annualised.","This is the in-house (soft) share — no cost is removed there.","Counted every avoided visit as hard, including in-house visits that remove no spend.",""],trap:"Freed capacity is soft unless a budget line is actually removed.",bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-309",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A project delivers first-year hard savings of **OMR 90,000**. Its total first-year cost is **OMR 30,000**. What is the first-year **ROI**?",options:["200%","300%","33.3%","66.7%"],answer:0,hint:"ROI = (benefit − cost) divided by cost, × 100.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Benefit = 90,000; cost = 30,000."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{ROI}=\\dfrac{\\text{Benefit}-\\text{Cost}}{\\text{Cost}}\\times 100=\\dfrac{90{,}000-30{,}000}{30{,}000}\\times 100=\\dfrac{60{,}000}{30{,}000}\\times 100=200\\%$$"}],final:"ROI = 200% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Benefit ÷ cost × 100 — the cost was not subtracted first.","Cost ÷ benefit — the ratio is upside down.","Divided the net benefit by the benefit instead of by the cost."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-310",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Finance will validate savings only if monthly **SLA penalty costs** (OMR thousands) are statistically lower after the change. The data are skewed, so the Belt runs a **Mann-Whitney** test on 12 months before and 12 months after:

\`\`\`
Mann-Whitney: Before, After

Descriptive Statistics
Sample   N  Median
Before  12   14.60
After   12    9.10

Estimation for Difference
Difference  CI for Difference  Achieved Confidence
      5.35      (2.90, 7.80)               95.44%

Test
Null hypothesis        H0: η1 - η2 = 0
Alternative hypothesis H1: η1 - η2 > 0

Method                  W-Value  P-Value
Not adjusted for ties    204.00    0.003
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, what is the correct conclusion?`,options:["Accept H0: the medians are equal, so no saving can be booked","Fail to reject H0, because the p-value is below 0.05","Reject H0: the median monthly penalty is lower after the change, which statistically supports the claimed saving","Reject H0, which proves the full annual saving has already been realised"],answer:2,hint:"p < α → reject H0. A significant test supports validation; realisation needs sustained tracking.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"p = 0.003 < 0.05 → **reject H0**. The one-sided alternative says Before is higher than After. The CI for the difference (2.90 to 7.80) is entirely above 0."},{title:"Apply to benefits",body:"This gives Finance statistical support to **validate** the saving. It becomes **realised** only after the gain lasts and the money shows up — tracked for about 12 months."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Never “accept” H0; a small p-value means reject, not fail to reject; and a test does not show the money is already realised."}],final:"p = 0.003 < 0.05 → reject H0 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Wrong on two counts: H0 is never “accepted”, and p = 0.003 leads to rejecting it.","Reversed decision rule: p below α means reject H0.","","Statistical significance supports validation; realised savings need sustained tracking in Control."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-311",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`A Green Belt totals the first-year benefits she claims:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Benefit type; Sum of OMR

Benefit type        Sum
Hard saving       84000
Soft saving       52000
Cost avoidance    30000
All              166000
\`\`\`

Which amount should appear as a reduction of **actual spend** in the profit and loss?`,options:["OMR 166,000","OMR 136,000","OMR 114,000","OMR 84,000"],answer:3,hint:"Only hard savings reduce actual spend against a baseline.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Hard savings** are a measurable fall in actual spending. **Soft savings** free capacity; **cost avoidance** prevents a future cost that is not in today’s spend."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Only the Hard saving row — **OMR 84,000** — lowers actual spend."}],final:"Hard savings = OMR 84,000 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Adds soft savings and cost avoidance, which do not reduce today’s spend.","Adds soft savings (84,000 + 52,000); freed capacity is not a fall in spend.","Adds cost avoidance (84,000 + 30,000); avoided future costs are not in current spend.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-312",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`After go-live in month 13, the stage centre line of monthly repair cost fell from **52.0** to **41.5** (OMR thousands). Finance plans to book a full year of realised savings based on the new centre line. The I chart session shows:

\`\`\`
Test Results for I Chart of Cost by Stage

TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  22

TEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.
Test Failed at points:  (none)
\`\`\`

What is the BEST response?`,options:["Investigate the special cause at month 22 before confirming the realised figure; book only what the actual months show","Book the full saving — one point is not important","Delete month 22 from the data and recalculate the centre line","Cancel the benefit, because a single test failure proves the change did not work"],answer:0,hint:"Test 1 = special cause. Realised benefits must reflect what actually happened, and gains must be sustained.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Month 22 is beyond 3σ of the After-stage centre line — a **special-cause** signal. Test 2 shows no sustained shift."},{title:"Apply to benefits",body:"Realised benefits must match **actual** results. The team should find out what happened in month 22 (a one-off event or the start of the gains slipping) before Finance confirms the figure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring or deleting the point hides the evidence; one point alone does not prove the change failed."}],final:"Investigate the special cause, then book actual savings → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A Test 1 failure is a special-cause signal; booking a full saving without checking it overstates the benefit.","Deleting an inconvenient point without finding its cause is not honest benefits capture.","One special-cause point needs investigation, but it does not prove the whole improvement failed."],bookRef:"Book p. 577"},{id:"sv-1-313",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Contractor repair costs fell **20%** in the six months after a process change. During the same period, the operator’s **largest corporate customer** — about a quarter of all repair tickets — moved to a competitor. How should the Green Belt report the benefit?",options:["Claim the full 20% fall, because costs really did go down","Claim no benefit, because the customer loss makes all the data useless","Agree with Finance a normalised method (e.g. cost per ticket) so that only the part caused by the project is claimed","Wait until the customer returns and then measure again"],answer:2,hint:"Attribute benefits honestly: remove effects the project did not cause, using a method agreed with Finance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Benefits capture must **attribute honestly**. A drop in volume because a customer left is not a project benefit."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Normalising (cost per ticket, or cost per 1,000 lines) separates the volume effect from the process effect. The method is **agreed with Finance**, the independent evaluator of savings."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Claiming everything overstates the benefit; claiming nothing or waiting ignores a real effect that can be measured."}],final:"Normalise and claim only the project’s share → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Part of the fall came from lost volume, not the project — claiming it all overstates the benefit.","The data are still usable once normalised by volume; the project’s real effect would be lost.","","The customer may never return; benefits should be measured now with a fair, normalised method."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-314",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A project fixes a data-retention gap. The regulator had warned that the gap would lead to a fine of about **OMR 50,000** next year. The fine has never been paid and is not in any budget. How should Finance classify this benefit?",options:["Hard saving","Soft saving","Cost avoidance","Revenue growth"],answer:2,hint:"Cost avoidance prevents a future cost that is not in current spending.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Cost avoidance** prevents a **future** cost that has not yet been paid. It is valuable, but today’s spending does not fall."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The fine is expected next year and is not in the budget, so preventing it is cost avoidance."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A hard saving reduces actual spend against a baseline; a soft saving frees capacity; revenue growth is extra income."}],final:"Prevented future fine = cost avoidance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Hard savings reduce actual spending against a baseline; this fine was never in the spend.","Soft savings are indirect gains such as freed hours; an avoided fine is a prevented future cost.","","Revenue growth is additional income; no new income is created here."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-315",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The charter shows *“Estimated benefit: OMR 120,000 per year”*. When does this become a **realised** benefit?",options:["When the Champion signs the charter at the Define tollgate","When the pilot in Improve shows the expected effect","After Control, when the gain has lasted and the money has actually appeared, tracked for about 12 months","When the Measure phase confirms the baseline"],answer:2,hint:"Estimated (Define) → validated (after Improve) → realised (after Control).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Benefits move through three stages: **estimated** in the charter, **validated** when pilot data show the real effect, and **realised** only when the gain has been **sustained** and the money has appeared — usually tracked month by month for about a year after Control."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Signing the charter only approves an estimate; the pilot validates it; the baseline in Measure sets the starting point."}],final:"Realised = after Control, tracked ~12 months → answer **C**",whyWrong:["At the Define tollgate the benefit is only an estimate.","A successful pilot validates the benefit; realisation needs the gain to last in daily operation.","","The Measure baseline is the starting point, not proof that any money has been saved."],bookRef:"Book p. 577"},{id:"sv-1-316",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"On the book’s DMAIC roadmap, which step closes the **Control** phase, just before “Go to Next Project”?",options:["Verify Financial Impact","Calculate the cost of poor quality","Develop the control plan","Validate the measurement system"],answer:0,hint:"The roadmap ends by confirming the money.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s roadmap ends Control with **“Verify Financial Impact”**, then “Go to Next Project”. Its Control roadblocks include financial benefits that are **not tracked and integrated into the business**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"COPQ is estimated in Define, the measurement system is validated in Measure, and the control plan is built earlier in Control."}],final:"Last Control step = Verify Financial Impact → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The COPQ estimate is a Define step that feeds the business case.","The control plan is developed during Control, before the final financial check.","Measurement-system validation (MSA) is a Measure step."],bookRef:"Book p. 499"},{id:"sv-1-317",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"All of the following are **hard savings** EXCEPT:",options:["Lower monthly invoices from a repair contractor paid per visit","Not buying a planned server next year that is not yet in any budget","Fewer SLA service credits paid to enterprise customers","An overtime budget line that is removed from the cost centre"],answer:1,hint:"Hard = actual spend falls against a baseline. A future purchase that never happens is cost avoidance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Hard savings** are a measurable fall in **current** spending. **Cost avoidance** prevents a **future** cost."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Contractor invoices, SLA credits and a removed overtime budget all lower actual spend. The unbudgeted server was never being paid for, so not buying it is cost avoidance."}],final:"The avoided future purchase is NOT hard → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Contractor invoices really fall — a hard saving.","","Credits paid out really fall — a hard saving.","Removing a budget line lowers actual spend — a hard saving."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-318",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these benefits-capture activities in the usual order:

- **P** Hand over the control plan to the process owner at the Control tollgate
- **Q** Estimate the benefit in the charter from the baseline cost of poor quality
- **R** Track realised savings monthly for about 12 months, with Finance recording the figure
- **S** Validate the benefit with pilot data after Improve, agreeing the method with Finance`,options:["S – Q – P – R","Q – P – S – R","Q – S – R – P","Q – S – P – R"],answer:3,hint:"Estimated → validated → handed over → realised.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Benefits are **estimated** in Define (Q), **validated** after Improve (S), the solution is **handed over** in Control (P), and the savings are then **realised** and tracked for about a year (R)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Order: **Q – S – P – R**."}],final:"Q – S – P – R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Validation needs pilot data, which cannot exist before the charter estimate.","Handover in Control cannot come before validation with Improve pilot data.","Realised savings are tracked after the handover, not before it.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 577–578"},{id:"sv-1-319",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A number-change request receives **30 minutes** of value-added work. Its total lead time, from request to completion, is **5 hours**. What is the **process cycle efficiency** (value-added time as a share of total lead time)?",options:["11.1%","10%","90%","600%"],answer:1,hint:"Process efficiency = value-added time divided by TOTAL lead time, in the same time unit.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"VA time = 30 min. Lead time = 5 h = **300 min** (convert to the same unit first)."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{PCE}=\\dfrac{\\text{VA time}}{\\text{Total lead time}}=\\dfrac{30}{300}=0.10=10\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"90% of the lead time adds no value — mostly waiting. The biggest gains come from removing that waiting, not from speeding up the 30 minutes of work."}],final:"PCE = 10% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided VA time by non-value-added time (30 ÷ 270) instead of by the total lead time.","","This is the non-value-added share of the lead time, not the efficiency.","Mixed units: 30 minutes divided by 5 hours as if both were the same unit."],trap:"Convert to one time unit, and divide by the TOTAL lead time.",bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-320",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team classifies every step of the **business-internet order** process (one order traced end to end):

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Step type; Count, Sum of Minutes

Step type   Count   Minutes
VA              4        35
BVA             3        25
NVA             9      1380
All            16      1440
\`\`\`

The BVA steps include a credit check required by company policy. Which improvement direction is BEST?`,options:["Speed up the four value-added steps, because they are what the customer pays for","Remove all three BVA steps, including the credit check, because the customer does not pay for them","Add a final inspection step so that errors are caught before activation","Attack the non-value-added steps (1,380 of 1,440 minutes), and minimise but keep the required BVA steps"],answer:3,hint:"NVA → eliminate; BVA (required) → minimise, do not delete; VA → keep and improve.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"NVA steps take **1,380 of 1,440 minutes** — about 96% of the lead time. VA work is only 35 minutes."},{title:"Apply Lean",body:"The big gain is in removing **NVA** waste (queues, hand-offs, rework). **BVA** steps such as a required credit check are **minimised and simplified**, not deleted."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Speeding up 35 minutes of VA work changes little; an extra inspection adds another non-value-added step."}],final:"Remove NVA, minimise BVA → answer **D**",whyWrong:["VA work is only 35 of 1,440 minutes; even halving it barely changes the lead time.","BVA steps are required by the business (policy, law, contract); they are minimised, not removed.","Inspection does not change form, fit or function — it adds a non-value-added step.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-321",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Before a **SIM swap**, the agent must verify the customer’s civil ID, as required by the telecom regulator. The customer does not value this step and would not pay for it. How should a Lean team treat it?",options:["As business-value-added: keep it, but simplify and shorten it as much as possible","As value-added: it changes the service, so improve and expand it","As non-value-added waste: eliminate it to cut the lead time","As a constraint: leave it out of the process map"],answer:0,hint:"Required by law or contract, but not valued by the customer → BVA → minimise, do not delete.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **business-value-added (BVA)** step fails the customer’s value test but is **required** (legal, regulatory, safety, contract). The book notes that some non-value activities, such as those needed for regulations, **cannot be removed**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The ID check is a regulatory requirement → BVA. The team keeps it but makes it as quick and simple as possible (e.g. digital ID scan)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Checking does not change form, fit or function, so it is not VA; and removing a legal requirement is not an option."}],final:"Required but not valued → BVA → minimise → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A check does not change form, fit or function of the service, so it is not value-added.","The step is a legal requirement; it cannot simply be eliminated.","Required steps must still appear on the process map; leaving them off hides real lead time."],bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-322",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Every agent on a service desk is busy 100% of the time, yet customers wait days for answers. Which Lean idea BEST explains this?",options:["Agents need more training, so the value-added work takes longer than it should","The desk is overstaffed, so work is spread too thinly","Lean follows the work item, not the resource: tickets can wait in queues between busy people, so high utilisation does not mean good flow","High utilisation always means the process is efficient; the delay must come from the customer"],answer:2,hint:"“Follow the ticket, not the engineer.” Resource efficiency ≠ flow efficiency.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Traditional management watches the **resource** (is the agent busy?). Lean watches the **work item** (where is the ticket, and is anyone working on it?)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Busy agents can still leave tickets waiting in queues and hand-offs. Lead time is mostly waiting, so improving **flow** — not keeping people busier — shortens it."}],final:"Follow the work item: utilisation ≠ flow → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Training may help the work itself, but the delay comes from tickets waiting between steps.","If agents are 100% busy, they are not overstaffed; the issue is flow, not headcount.","","High utilisation often goes with long queues; the delay is inside the process, not with the customer."],bookRef:"Book p. 590"},{id:"sv-1-323",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"The book’s **Lean tools pyramid** includes all of the following levels EXCEPT:",options:["5S","Visual Factory","Kanban","Design of Experiments"],answer:3,hint:"Pyramid from the base: 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s pyramid runs from the base up: **5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban**, with sustaining results as the goal. Each level needs the one below it."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"5S, Visual Factory and Kanban are all levels. **Design of Experiments** is a statistical Improve tool, not a level of the Lean pyramid."}],final:"DOE is NOT in the Lean pyramid → answer **D**",whyWrong:["5S is the base of the pyramid.","Visual Factory sits directly above 5S.","Kanban is the top level of the pyramid.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-324",phase:1,topic:"1.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Who **first used the term “lean”** to describe the Toyota way of working, in a 1988 article from MIT’s car-industry research programme?",options:["James Womack and Daniel Jones","John Krafcik","Taiichi Ohno","Eiji Toyoda"],answer:1,hint:"Coined in 1988 → popularised in 1990 → five principles in 1996.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In **1988** MIT researcher **John Krafcik** called the Toyota system “lean” because it used less of everything — effort, space, inventory and time."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Womack and Jones **popularised** the term (*The Machine That Changed the World*, 1990, with Roos) and later wrote *Lean Thinking* (1996). Ohno built the Toyota Production System; Eiji Toyoda led Toyota."}],final:"Krafcik coined “lean” in 1988 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Womack and Jones spread the word through their 1990 and 1996 books; they did not coin it.","","Ohno created the Toyota Production System but did not use the English word “lean”.","Eiji Toyoda led Toyota and backed TPS; the term came from MIT research."],trap:"Coined (Krafcik, 1988) ≠ popularised (Womack, Jones and Roos, 1990).",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.4.2"},{id:"sv-1-325",phase:1,topic:"1.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"**Sakichi Toyoda** invented a loom that **stopped automatically** when a thread broke, so one worker could watch many machines and no defective cloth was made. Which Toyota Production System idea grew from this?",options:["Kanban","Just-in-Time","SMED","Jidoka"],answer:3,hint:"TPS pillars: JIT (right amount, right time) and Jidoka (stop and fix, build quality in).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Jidoka** — “automation with a human touch” — means a process **stops itself** when something abnormal happens, so the problem is fixed at its source and not passed on."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Just-in-Time (the other pillar) is about producing only what is needed when needed; kanban is its signalling card; SMED is Shingo’s quick-changeover method."}],final:"Self-stopping loom → Jidoka → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Kanban is the signal card used for pull production, not the stop-on-defect idea.","Just-in-Time is the other TPS pillar — producing only what is needed, when needed.","SMED is Shigeo Shingo’s quick-changeover method.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.4.2"},{id:"sv-1-326",phase:1,topic:"1.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"**Taiichi Ohno** was inspired by American **supermarkets**, which refill a shelf only after customers take goods from it. Which Lean idea does this describe?",options:["Pull production — replenish only what has been used","Push production to a sales forecast","The 5S workplace method","Poka-yoke (mistake-proofing)"],answer:0,hint:"Supermarket shelf → replenish on use → pull, signalled by kanban.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The supermarket refills only what customers take. Ohno used this for **pull** production within **Just-in-Time**: the next step signals what it has used (with a **kanban** card) and only that is made."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Forecast-driven push is the opposite of pull. 5S is workplace organisation, and poka-yoke is mistake-proofing."}],final:"Supermarket model → pull → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Making to a forecast is push — exactly what the supermarket model replaces.","5S organises the workplace; it is not about replenishment.","Poka-yoke prevents errors; it is not about how much to make or when."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-1-327",phase:1,topic:"1.4.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these Lean milestones in **chronological** order:

- **P** Taiichi Ohno publishes *Toyota Production System* in Japanese
- **Q** Henry Ford starts the moving assembly line for the Model T
- **R** John Krafcik uses the word “lean” for the Toyota system
- **S** Eiji Toyoda visits Ford’s US plants and decides Toyota needs a different system`,options:["Q – P – S – R","S – Q – P – R","Q – S – P – R","Q – S – R – P"],answer:2,hint:"1913 Ford → 1950 Toyoda visit → 1978 Ohno’s book → 1988 “lean”.",steps:[{title:"Place each event",body:"Ford’s moving line **1913** (Q); Eiji Toyoda’s visit **1950** (S); Ohno’s book **1978** (P); Krafcik’s “lean” **1988** (R)."},{title:"Order",body:"**Q – S – P – R**."}],final:"Q – S – P – R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Ohno’s 1978 book came well after Eiji Toyoda’s 1950 visit to Ford.","The Toyoda visit (1950) studied Ford’s line, which already existed from 1913.","","Krafcik coined “lean” in 1988, ten years after Ohno’s 1978 book."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.4.2"},{id:"sv-1-328",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"For the **same fault type**, restore times range from **40 minutes to 9 hours** depending on shift and engineer. Nobody knows why, and five months of ticket data are available. Which approach should the Green Belt lead with?",options:["A Lean kaizen event to remove a visible queue","A Six Sigma DMAIC project to find the hidden sources of variation","Just do it: retrain all engineers this week","A 5S event in the network operations centre"],answer:1,hint:"Waste makes results slow; variation makes them unpredictable. Unknown cause + variation + data → DMAIC.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Six Sigma** targets **variation and defects** with a data-driven DMAIC structure. **Lean** targets visible waste and slow flow."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The problem is **inconsistency** (40 min to 9 h) with an **unknown cause** and good data — the classic case for DMAIC to find which X’s (shift, engineer, fault detail…) drive the spread."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No visible queue is described, retraining is a guess at the cause, and 5S does not address unexplained variation."}],final:"Unexplained variation → Six Sigma DMAIC → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A kaizen suits obvious, visible waste; here the cause of variation is hidden.","","Retraining assumes the cause; “just do it” is only for known causes with obvious fixes.","5S organises the workplace; it does not explain why restore times vary so widely."],bookRef:"Book p. 501"},{id:"sv-1-329",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Enterprise leased-line orders take **15 days** on average, most of it waiting between teams, **and** delivery times are very inconsistent (4 to 40 days). The problem costs major contracts. What is the BEST approach?",options:["Lean only, because most of the time is waiting","Six Sigma only, because delivery times are inconsistent","Lean Six Sigma: DMAIC as the backbone, with Lean tools used inside it to remove waiting","Two separate projects — Lean on the first half of the process and Six Sigma on the second"],answer:2,hint:"Slow AND inconsistent, high impact → Lean Six Sigma: DMAIC backbone + Lean tools.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Lean shortens the **average** (waste, waiting); Six Sigma narrows the **spread** (variation). When a process is both slow and inconsistent, **Lean Six Sigma** uses both."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"DMAIC gives the structure and statistics for the variation; Lean tools (value stream map, flow, pull) remove the waiting inside the same project."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Using only one lens leaves half the problem. Splitting the process between methods breaks the end-to-end view."}],final:"Slow + inconsistent → Lean Six Sigma → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Removing waiting would shorten the average but not explain the 4-to-40-day spread.","Reducing variation alone would leave most of the waiting in place.","","Lean and Six Sigma are partners on the same process, not tools for separate halves."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-330",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Think of the distribution of a process’s lead times. Which statement BEST describes what Lean and Six Sigma each mainly do to it?",options:["Lean narrows the spread; Six Sigma shortens the average","Both only shorten the average","Both only narrow the spread","Lean shortens the average by removing waste; Six Sigma narrows the spread by reducing variation"],answer:3,hint:"Waste → slow average; variation → unpredictable spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Waste** (waiting, rework loops, hand-offs) makes the average lead time long → Lean moves the **average**. **Variation** makes results unpredictable → Six Sigma narrows the **spread**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Together they move the whole distribution inside the customer’s limit — which is why the book calls them partners."}],final:"Lean = average, Six Sigma = spread → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Reversed: Lean removes waste (average); Six Sigma reduces variation (spread).","Six Sigma’s main target is variation, not only the average.","Lean’s main target is waste and flow time, not only the spread.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-331",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Two processes are reviewed. Customer requirement: refunds within **5 days**; device upgrades within **3 days**.

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Lead time (days)

Process     N   Mean  StDev  Minimum  Maximum
Refunds    80  10.20   0.45     9.10    11.30
Upgrades   80   2.10   1.85     0.20     9.80
\`\`\`

Which lens fits each process BEST?`,options:["Refunds → Lean (consistently slow); Upgrades → Six Sigma (average fine, spread too wide)","Refunds → Six Sigma; Upgrades → Lean","Both → Lean, because both have long maximum times","Both → Six Sigma, because both miss the requirement"],answer:0,hint:"Small StDev but mean far off → waste (Lean). Mean fine but StDev large → variation (Six Sigma).",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Refunds: mean 10.2 days, StDev only 0.45 — **every** refund is slow, and consistently so. Upgrades: mean 2.1 days (inside 3), but StDev 1.85 and a maximum of 9.8 — **unpredictable**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Consistent slowness points to built-in **waste** (waiting, hand-offs) → Lean. A good average with a wide spread points to **variation** → Six Sigma."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The maximum alone does not show the problem type; read the mean and StDev together."}],final:"Refunds = waste (Lean), Upgrades = variation (Six Sigma) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Reversed: refunds are consistent but slow (waste), upgrades are fast on average but variable (variation).","Upgrades have a good average; their problem is spread, which needs Six Sigma.","Refunds vary very little; their issue is built-in delay, which Lean attacks first."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-332",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the course book, *muda* means waste, and the presence of muda shows that defects are occurring in the process. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Book p. 502: reducing muda can reduce outliers and help prevent defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book links waste and defects: outliers arise from **differing waste** among procedures, machines and people, so reducing **muda** can reduce outliers and help prevent defects. Its quiz states that muda means waste, which shows defects are occurring."},{title:"Apply",body:"The statement matches the book, so it is **true**. This link is one reason Lean and Six Sigma work as partners."}],final:"Matches the book → **True** (answer **A**)",whyWrong:["","The book explicitly ties muda (waste) to defects and outliers; waste and variation are linked, not separate."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-333",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A depot has **20** field technicians. Each does **8** jobs a day and spends about **12 minutes per job** searching the van and depot for the right tools. How many **technician-hours per day** does this searching cost?",options:["1.6 hours","1,920 hours","32 hours","4 hours"],answer:2,hint:"Total time = minutes per job × jobs per person × people; divide minutes by 60 for hours.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"12 min per job; 8 jobs per technician per day; 20 technicians."},{title:"Pick the formula and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Hours lost}=\\dfrac{12\\times 8\\times 20}{60}=\\dfrac{1{,}920}{60}=32$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Searching is the waste of **Motion**. 32 technician-hours a day is roughly four full-time people — a strong case for 5S on the vans."}],final:"32 technician-hours a day → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is one technician only (96 minutes); the 20 technicians were left out.","This is the total in minutes (1,920), not converted to hours.","","Left out the 8 jobs per day: 12 × 20 = 240 minutes = 4 hours."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-334",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Gemba walk in the enterprise sales back office logs the minutes lost each day:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Observation; Sum of Minutes lost per day

Observation                                          Minutes
Agents idle until the credit-check result arrives        150
Re-keying order data already captured in the web form    100
Searching the shared drive for contract templates         95
Walking to the scanner on another floor                   80
Fixing orders rejected for missing fields                 60
All                                                      485
\`\`\`

Grouped by the seven elements of waste, which waste costs the MOST time?`,options:["Waiting","Motion","Overprocessing","Correction"],answer:1,hint:"Classify each row first, then add rows that belong to the same waste.",steps:[{title:"Classify each row",body:"Idle until the credit check = **Waiting** (150). Re-keying data already captured = duplicate work = **Overprocessing** (100). Searching the drive and walking to the scanner = people moving and searching = **Motion** (95 + 80). Fixing rejected orders = **Correction** (60)."},{title:"Add by waste",body:"$$\\text{Motion}=95+80=175$$ Waiting = 150, Overprocessing = 100, Correction = 60. Motion is the largest."},{title:"Trap",body:"The biggest single row is Waiting (150), but two rows together make Motion larger."}],final:"Motion = 175 min a day → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Waiting is the largest single row (150), but Motion totals 175 once its two rows are added.","","Overprocessing (re-keying) is 100 minutes — less than Motion.","Correction is 60 minutes; even adding the re-keying it would be 160, still below Motion."],trap:"Group by waste before ranking — the largest row is not always the largest waste.",bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-335",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A NOC tracks a daily snapshot of its fault queue:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Tickets awaiting assignment (daily snapshot)

Variable   N   Mean  StDev  Minimum  Maximum
Queued    30  640.0   85.0    480.0    810.0
\`\`\`

Viewed from the **work item’s** side, this metric mainly measures which waste?`,options:["Inventory","Waiting","Overproduction","Conveyance"],answer:0,hint:"A quantity of items sitting in the system = Inventory; a person or customer idle = Waiting.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Waiting and Inventory are two views of the same queue. A **count of items sitting** in the process (work-in-progress) is **Inventory**. People or customers **idle** for something is **Waiting**."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The metric counts tickets (a mean of 640) sitting unassigned — a pile of work-in-progress → Inventory. It ties up capacity and hides problems."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Waiting would be measured in time from the customer’s or engineer’s view; overproduction is making too much or too early; conveyance is moving items between locations."}],final:"A queue count = Inventory → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Waiting is the time view (a customer or engineer idle); this output is a count of items sitting in the queue.","Nothing is being produced ahead of demand; the tickets are real faults waiting for work.","Conveyance is moving items between locations; these tickets are not moving at all."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-336",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"On every ticket, agents must fill in **three free-text summary fields**. No downstream team reads them, and the customer gains nothing from them. Which waste is this?",options:["Overproduction","Correction","Motion","Overprocessing"],answer:3,hint:"Overprocessing = more work on each item than the customer needs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Overprocessing** is doing more work per item, or to a higher standard, than the customer needs — extra fields, duplicate entry, extra approvals."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Each ticket gets extra effort that adds nothing for the customer or any downstream step."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Overproduction is about **quantity or timing** (too many, too soon), not effort per item. No error is fixed (Correction) and nobody moves (Motion)."}],final:"Extra work per item → Overprocessing → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Overproduction means too many items or made too early; here it is extra effort on each item.","Correction is fixing errors; nothing is being corrected.","Motion is people moving or searching; typing extra fields is extra processing.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-337",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A fault ticket is passed from the service desk to the **access** team, then to the **transport** team, then back to **access** before anyone fixes it. Which waste does the repeated **movement of the ticket** represent?",options:["Motion","Conveyance","Waiting","Overprocessing"],answer:1,hint:"Conveyance = materials or information moved between locations; Motion = people moving within the workplace.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Conveyance** is moving **materials, products or information** between locations more than needed — many hand-offs, double handling."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The ticket (information) is bounced between three groups. Nobody is walking; the work item is being transported."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Waiting will occur between hand-offs, but the question asks about the movement itself. Motion is about people."}],final:"Information moved between groups → Conveyance → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Motion is people moving within the workplace; here the ticket moves, not a person.","","The ticket will wait in each queue, but the stem asks about the movement between teams.","No extra work is done on the ticket at each stop; it is just passed along."],trap:"Ask what moves: a person → Motion; a thing or information → Conveyance.",bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-338",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Enterprise customers’ accounts-payable teams reject many invoices because the **purchase-order number is missing**. The billing team must correct and re-issue each one. Which waste is this?",options:["Correction","Inventory","Overprocessing","Conveyance"],answer:0,hint:"Correction = effort spent fixing errors (defects, rework, re-issue).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Correction** is the effort spent **fixing errors** — the defect plus the rework, re-checking and re-issuing it causes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A missing PO number is a defect; correcting and re-issuing the invoice is rework. The book’s hint applies: “re-” activities (re-issue) are usually waste."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A pile of rejected invoices would be Inventory, but the question is about the fixing work."}],final:"Fixing defective invoices → Correction → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Inventory is a quantity of items sitting in the system; the stem describes the work of fixing them.","Overprocessing is extra work beyond what the customer needs on a good item; here the item is defective.","Conveyance is moving items between locations; the problem is the error and its rework."],bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-339",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"To “save time”, the installation team builds and configures customer-premises kits for business orders **before** the credit check is complete. About **25%** of these orders then fail the credit check, and their kits must be stripped down again. Which waste is the ROOT problem?",options:["Inventory","Correction","Conveyance","Overproduction"],answer:3,hint:"Overproduction = making more, or EARLIER, than the next step needs. It creates other wastes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Overproduction** is making more **or earlier** than the next step needs. It is often called the worst waste because it **creates** the others — stock, handling and rework."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Kits are built before demand is confirmed (credit check). The kits waiting on shelves (Inventory) and the stripping-down work (Correction-like rework) are **consequences** of building too early."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The question asks for the **root** waste, not its symptoms."}],final:"Building before demand is confirmed → Overproduction → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The kits waiting on shelves are inventory, but that stock exists only because they were built too early.","Stripping kits down is rework caused by the early build — a symptom, not the root.","Nothing in the stem is about moving kits between locations.",""],trap:"Overproduction includes making things too EARLY, not only too many.",bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-340",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A technician arrives at a customer site, installs the router and then sits idle for **25 minutes** until the NOC activates the port remotely. Which waste is this?",options:["Motion","Inventory","Waiting","Conveyance"],answer:2,hint:"Waiting = people, equipment or work idle because something upstream is not ready.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Waiting** is when people, equipment or work are **idle** because something else is not ready. It is measured in time and directly stretches the lead time."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The technician is idle until the remote activation is done — classic waiting."}],final:"Technician idle for activation → Waiting → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Motion is unnecessary movement; the technician is not moving at all.","Inventory is a quantity of items sitting in the system, not a person who is idle.","","Conveyance is moving materials or information between locations."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-341",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"The central warehouse holds about **nine months** of home routers, several older models that are no longer sold, and boxes of spare parts nobody has counted. Which waste does this stock MOST directly represent?",options:["Overproduction","Conveyance","Overprocessing","Inventory"],answer:3,hint:"Inventory = more material, information or work-in-progress than is needed right now.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Inventory** is holding more material or work-in-progress than is needed now. It ties up cash and space, risks items becoming obsolete, and hides problems."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Nine months of stock, obsolete models and uncounted spares sitting in a warehouse are inventory waste."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Over-buying may have caused it, but the question asks what the stock **represents**: Inventory."}],final:"Excess stock → Inventory → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Overproduction is making too much or too early; the question asks what the sitting stock itself represents.","Conveyance would be moving the stock around; here it is just sitting.","Overprocessing is extra work on each item, not holding too many items.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-342",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which pair of definitions correctly separates **Motion** from **Conveyance**?",options:["Motion: people moving or searching within the workplace. Conveyance: materials or information moved between locations.","Motion: materials or information moved between locations. Conveyance: people moving within the workplace.","Motion: people idle while waiting for work. Conveyance: work items sitting in a queue.","Motion and Conveyance are two names for the same waste."],answer:0,hint:"Ask what moves: a person (Motion) or a thing / information (Conveyance).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Motion** = unnecessary movement of **people** (or equipment) within the work area: walking, reaching, searching, clicking through screens. **Conveyance** = moving **materials, products or information** between locations."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The second option swaps the two. Idle people are Waiting and queued items are Inventory. They are separate elements of the seven."}],final:"Person moves = Motion; thing moves = Conveyance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The definitions are swapped.","These describe Waiting and Inventory, not Motion and Conveyance.","They are two separate elements of the seven wastes."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-343",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Lean trainers often compare inventory to **water covering rocks**. What is the point of this picture?",options:["Inventory is like water: it flows naturally to where it is needed","Inventory should be increased so that the process never runs dry","High inventory hides problems (defects, unreliable steps, imbalances); lowering it makes them visible so they can be fixed","Inventory is a safe waste because it protects customers from every problem"],answer:2,hint:"Lower the water level and the rocks (problems) appear.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Extra stock and work-in-progress act as a buffer that **hides problems** — unreliable machines, defects, poor balance between steps. When inventory is lowered, those problems surface and can be solved."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The image is a warning, not praise: more inventory hides more problems and ties up cash."}],final:"Inventory hides problems → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The metaphor is about hiding problems, not about flow.","Raising inventory hides even more problems; Lean aims to lower it.","","Inventory is a waste; it hides problems rather than protecting against them."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-344",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The book gives a quick hint for spotting waste on a process map: activities whose names begin with which prefix are often waste?",options:["“pre-”","“re-”","“auto-”","“co-”"],answer:1,hint:"Rework, re-enter, re-check, re-route…",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that activities starting with **“re-”** — rework, replace, review, re-enter, re-route — have a good chance of being waste."},{title:"Apply",body:"Re-keying data is often overprocessing; re-issuing an invoice is correction; re-routing a ticket is conveyance."}],final:"“re-” activities signal waste → answer **B**",whyWrong:["“pre-” steps (pre-checks, preparation) are not singled out by the book.","","“auto-” (automatic) steps are not the book’s warning sign.","“co-” (coordinate, collaborate) is not the book’s waste signal."],bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-345",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are examples of the waste of **Motion** EXCEPT:",options:["A technician walking back to the van several times per job to fetch tools","An agent clicking through seven screens to find one customer detail","A NOC engineer repeatedly reaching to a high shelf for spare modules","Spare modems trucked from the central warehouse to a regional depot and then to a second depot"],answer:3,hint:"Motion = people moving or searching within the workplace. Things moved between locations = Conveyance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Motion** is unnecessary movement of **people** within the work area — walking, reaching, searching, extra clicks."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Walking, clicking and reaching are all people moving. Trucking modems between sites is **materials** moving between locations → **Conveyance**."}],final:"Trucking modems is Conveyance, NOT Motion → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A person walking to and from the van is Motion.","Extra clicks and screen-searching are the office form of Motion.","Repeated reaching for items is Motion — a sign that Straighten (5S) is needed.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-346",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Excess **inventory** or work-in-progress causes all of the following EXCEPT:",options:["Cash tied up in stock or unfinished work","Problems hidden behind the buffer","Shorter lead times for each work item","Items becoming obsolete before they are used"],answer:2,hint:"More WIP in a queue → each item waits longer (Little’s Law).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Inventory ties up cash, hides problems and risks obsolescence. With the same throughput, **more work-in-progress means each item waits longer**, so lead time gets **longer**, not shorter."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Cash, hidden problems and obsolescence are real effects. Shorter lead time is the opposite of what excess inventory does."}],final:"Excess WIP lengthens lead time → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Stock and WIP do tie up cash — a real cost of inventory.","Inventory hides problems like water over rocks — a real effect.","","Obsolescence is a real risk of holding too much stock."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-347",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A field technician who walks back to the van several times per job to fetch tools is an example of the waste of **Conveyance**. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"A person moving within the workplace = Motion.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Motion** = people moving or searching within the workplace. **Conveyance** = materials or information moved between locations."},{title:"Apply",body:"A person walking to the van is **Motion**, so the statement is false."}],final:"It is Motion, not Conveyance → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["Conveyance is about moving things or information; a person walking is Motion.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-348",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A trainer maps the syllabus’s seven elements of waste to the names used in the **eight-waste** (DOWNTIME) list:

| Row | Syllabus 1.4.4 element | Eight-waste name |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Waiting | Non-utilized talent |
| 2 | Correction | Defects |
| 3 | Conveyance | Transportation |
| 4 | Overprocessing | Extra-processing |

Which row is **incorrectly** matched?`,options:["Row 1","Row 2","Row 3","Row 4"],answer:0,hint:"Three renames (Correction→Defects, Conveyance→Transportation, Overprocessing→Extra-processing) and one addition (Non-utilized talent).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The eight-waste list renames three of the seven elements and **adds** one new waste, **Non-utilized talent**, which has no match among the seven."},{title:"Check each row",body:"Rows 2–4 are the three correct renames. Waiting keeps the same name — **Waiting** — in both lists, so Row 1 is wrong."}],final:"Waiting ↔ Waiting, not Non-utilized talent → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Correction is called Defects in the eight-waste list — correct.","Conveyance is called Transportation in the eight-waste list — correct.","Overprocessing is called Extra-processing in the eight-waste list — correct."],trap:"Non-utilized talent is the extra eighth waste; it is not one of the seven.",bookRef:"Syllabus p. 2"},{id:"sv-1-349",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`During **Sort**, a team classifies every item in a network equipment room:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Sort decision

Decision   Count  Percent
Useful       140    46.67
Useless       95    31.67
Unknown       65    21.67
N=           300
\`\`\`

According to the book’s method, what should happen to the **65 Unknown** items?`,options:["Dispose of them immediately, together with the useless items","Keep them where they are for a set period, monitor their use, then dispose of those not used","Store all of them within arm’s reach in case they are needed","Re-classify them as useful so nothing is lost"],answer:1,hint:"Useless → dispose now; Unknown → keep and monitor for a period; Useful → store by frequency of use.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Sort method classifies items as **useful, useless or unknown**. Useless items go now. **Unknown** items are kept for a **set period** and their use is **monitored**; those not used are then disposed of."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The 65 Unknown items (21.67%) are often red-tagged and watched; the 95 Useless items can go at once."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Throwing them away now risks losing something needed; keeping them all near the bench defeats the purpose of Sort."}],final:"Unknown → keep and monitor for a set period → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Only the useless items go at once; unknown items are monitored first.","","Arm’s reach is for items used daily (class A); unknown items have not shown any use.","Re-labelling without evidence keeps the clutter Sort is meant to remove."],bookRef:"Book p. 505"},{id:"sv-1-350",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`After Sort, a NOC team records how often each useful item is used:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Frequency of use

Frequency              Count
Several times a day       18
Weekly                    25
Monthly                   40
Quarterly or less         12
N=                        95
\`\`\`

Using the book’s ABC frequency-of-use guide, how many items should be stored **within arm’s reach**?`,options:["43","95","18","83"],answer:2,hint:"A = daily or several times a day → arm’s reach; B = weekly → local; C = monthly or less → remote.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s guide: **class A** (daily or several times a day) → **arm’s reach**; **class B** (weekly) → a local cabinet or shelf; **class C** (monthly or quarterly) → remote storage."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Only the 18 items used several times a day are class A → **18** items within arm’s reach. The 25 weekly items go in local storage."}],final:"18 class-A items → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This adds the weekly items (18 + 25); weekly items belong in local storage, not at arm’s reach.","Keeping all 95 items at the bench would undo the Sort.","","This adds monthly items too (18 + 25 + 40); monthly items belong in remote storage."],bookRef:"Book p. 506"},{id:"sv-1-351",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"After cleaning the equipment room, the team draws a floor map that assigns **each zone to a named person** for regular cleaning, so that no corner becomes a “no man’s land”. They also trace why dust builds up near the door and fit a seal. Which 5S step is this?",options:["Sort","Straighten","Standardize","Shine"],answer:3,hint:"Shine = clean, inspect and find ways to keep it clean (mess prevention).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes **Shine** as cleaning the area **and finding ways to keep it clean**: prevent mess at its source, and use a layout map that gives each area a named cleaner, so there is no “no man’s land”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Zone ownership for cleaning and fixing the dust source are exactly these Shine actions."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Sort removes unneeded items; Straighten gives items a place; Standardize writes the rules and visual controls that keep the first three S’s in place."}],final:"Cleaning ownership + mess prevention → Shine → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Sort removes items that are not needed; nothing is removed here.","Straighten gives each item a labelled place; this is about cleaning.","Standardize sets the rules and visual controls; assigning cleaning zones and stopping dust at the source is part of Shine in the book.",""],trap:"The book places the cleaning-zone map and mess prevention under Shine.",bookRef:"Book p. 507"},{id:"sv-1-352",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After organising the racks, the team posts a **photo of the correct patch-panel layout** at every rack and adopts one **colour code** for cable types that all shifts must follow. Which 5S step is this?",options:["Straighten","Standardize","Shine","Self-Discipline"],answer:1,hint:"Standardize = rules and visual controls (signs, colour codes, photo standards) that keep the first three S’s in place.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Standardize** creates the **rules** that hold the first three S’s, kept by **visual controls**: signboards, painted lines, colour coding, photo standards."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A photo standard and a common colour code for all shifts define “what good looks like” — Standardize."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Straighten gives items a place; Self-Discipline is the habit of following the standards, checked by audits."}],final:"Photo standards and colour codes → Standardize → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Straighten gives each item its place; here the team defines the common standard for every shift.","","Shine is cleaning and inspection; no cleaning is described.","Self-Discipline is the ongoing habit of following the standard; this step creates the standard."],bookRef:"Book p. 507"},{id:"sv-1-353",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Six months after a 5S event, shift leads run a short **weekly 5S checklist**, results are reviewed at the monthly operations meeting, and the best-kept area is recognised. Which 5S step do these routines support?",options:["Standardize","Shine","Self-Discipline (Sustain)","Sort"],answer:2,hint:"Self-Discipline = the habit and commitment to follow the standards, kept alive by schedules, checklists and audits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book calls **Self-Discipline (Sustaining)** the discipline and commitment behind all the other stages — practise and repeat until it becomes a way of life — supported by **schedules and checklists**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Regular audits, management review and recognition keep the habit alive → Self-Discipline."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Standardize creates the rules; these routines check that people keep following them."}],final:"Audits and recognition keep the habit → Self-Discipline → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Standardize writes the rules and visual controls; checking and rewarding their use is Self-Discipline.","Shine is cleaning and inspecting the area.","","Sort removes unneeded items; it is the first step, not the sustaining one."],bookRef:"Book p. 508"},{id:"sv-1-354",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A service desk applies 5S to its ticket tool. As a first step it closes **400 stale duplicate tickets** and deletes queue categories that nobody has used for a year. Which 5S step is this?",options:["Sort","Straighten","Shine","Standardize"],answer:0,hint:"Sort = remove what is not needed for the current work.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Sort** removes everything not needed for current work and keeps only the essentials. It works the same way in a digital workplace."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Closing stale duplicates and deleting unused categories removes what is not needed → Sort."}],final:"Removing what is not needed → Sort → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Straighten gives the remaining items a clear place (e.g. a tidy category structure) after sorting.","Shine is regular cleaning and inspection; this is a one-off removal of unneeded items.","Standardize writes rules (e.g. naming conventions) to keep the result."],bookRef:"Book p. 504"},{id:"sv-1-355",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, how do the five S’s divide into two groups?",options:["The first three are sustaining actions; the last two are workplace actions","The first three are workplace actions; the last two are sustaining actions","The first two are planning actions; the last three are doing actions","All five are management actions that frontline staff do not take part in"],answer:1,hint:"Sort, Straighten, Shine act on the workplace; Standardize and Self-Discipline hold the gains.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the **first three S’s** (Sort, Straighten, Shine) are **workplace actions**, and the **last two** (Standardize, Self-Discipline) are **sustaining actions**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The first option swaps the groups. 5S depends on employees’ commitment, so it is not a management-only activity."}],final:"First three = workplace, last two = sustaining → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Reversed: the workplace actions come first.","","The book does not split 5S into planning and doing.","The book stresses that 5S depends on employees’ commitment; frontline staff are central to it."],bookRef:"Book p. 503"},{id:"sv-1-356",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book’s Lean tools pyramid, 5S is the base. Which level sits **directly above** 5S?",options:["Kanban","Kaizen","Standardized Work","Visual Factory"],answer:3,hint:"5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"From the base up: **5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban**. Each level needs the one below it."},{title:"Apply",body:"The visual factory is built on 5S standards — you cannot make problems visible in a cluttered space."}],final:"Visual Factory sits on 5S → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Kanban is at the top of the pyramid.","Kaizen is the fourth level, below Kanban.","Standardized Work is the third level, above the Visual Factory.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-357",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"The **Standardize** step relies on visual controls. All of the following are Standardize visual controls EXCEPT:",options:["Red tags placed on items whose use is unknown","Colour-coded zones for different equipment types","Photo standards showing the correct layout","Painted floor lines marking walkways and storage areas"],answer:0,hint:"Red tags belong to Sort (useful / useless / unknown).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says orderliness is the core of standardization, kept by **visual controls** — signboards, painted lines, colour coding, photo standards."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Colour zones, photo standards and painted lines are Standardize controls. **Red tags** mark items of unknown use during **Sort**."}],final:"Red tags belong to Sort → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Colour coding is a classic Standardize visual control.","Photo standards show “what good looks like” — Standardize.","Painted lines are a Standardize visual control named in the book."],bookRef:"Book p. 507"},{id:"sv-1-358",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The book recommends launching 5S across the whole organisation at once, rather than starting in a pilot area. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Start small, learn, then spread step by step.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book advises starting 5S in a **pilot area** and then spreading it step by step."},{title:"Apply",body:"A big-bang launch everywhere is the opposite of that advice, so the statement is false."}],final:"Start in a pilot area → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["The book recommends a pilot area first, then a step-by-step roll-out.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 503"},{id:"sv-1-359",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put the Japanese 5S terms in the correct order:

- **P** Seiso
- **Q** Seiri
- **R** Shitsuke
- **S** Seiton
- **T** Seiketsu`,options:["Q – S – P – T – R","S – Q – P – T – R","Q – P – S – T – R","Q – S – P – R – T"],answer:0,hint:"Seiri (Sort) → Seiton (Straighten) → Seiso (Shine) → Seiketsu (Standardize) → Shitsuke (Self-Discipline).",steps:[{title:"Map each term",body:"Seiri = **Sort** (Q); Seiton = **Straighten** (S); Seiso = **Shine** (P); Seiketsu = **Standardize** (T); Shitsuke = **Self-Discipline / Sustain** (R)."},{title:"Order",body:"**Q – S – P – T – R**."}],final:"Q – S – P – T – R → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Seiton (Straighten) cannot come before Seiri (Sort) — you first remove what is not needed.","Seiso (Shine) is placed before Seiton (Straighten); items get their place before cleaning routines are set.","Shitsuke (Self-Discipline) is last; it sustains the standards set in Seiketsu."],bookRef:"Book p. 504"},{id:"sv-1-360",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`A billing team applies **5S to its shared drive**. Put the activities in 5S order:

- **P** Agree a folder template and file-naming rule that everyone must use
- **Q** Delete obsolete and duplicate files
- **R** Team lead runs a quarterly review of the drive against the rules
- **S** Set up a clear folder structure so every file has one home
- **T** Regularly archive closed-project files and clear temporary folders`,options:["Q – T – S – P – R","S – Q – T – P – R","Q – S – P – T – R","Q – S – T – P – R"],answer:3,hint:"Sort → Straighten → Shine → Standardize → Self-Discipline.",steps:[{title:"Map each activity",body:"Delete obsolete files = **Sort** (Q). Folder structure = **Straighten** (S). Regular archiving and clean-up = **Shine** (T). Template and naming rule = **Standardize** (P). Quarterly review = **Self-Discipline** (R)."},{title:"Order",body:"**Q – S – T – P – R**."}],final:"Q – S – T – P – R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Shine (T) is placed before Straighten (S); files need a home before regular clean-up routines.","Straighten (S) before Sort (Q) organises files that should have been deleted.","Standardize (P) is placed before Shine (T); the 5S order is Shine, then Standardize.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 504"}];export{e as items};
